@page "/HowItWorks/same-day-rule"
@using InvestmentTaxCalculator.Components

<HowItWorksGuideLayout PageAddress="HowItWorks/same-day-rule">
    <ChildContent>
        <div class="section">
            <p>
                Under TCGA 1992 s105(1), all shares of the same class bought on one day count as a single purchase, and all
                shares sold on that day count as a single sale. The same-day match comes before every other rule. The app
                does this before any matching takes place:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>Trades are combined per day and per side.</strong> Every buy of a share on one calendar date
                    becomes one combined acquisition, and every sell becomes one combined disposal. Their quantities add up,
                    and so do their costs and proceeds. Fees are included (see
                    <a href="HowItWorks/net-proceeds" class="link-styled">Net proceeds and fees</a>).
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Time of day does not matter.</strong> A buy made after a sell on the same day is still matched
                    against it. The date used is the trade date in your broker statement. The app does not convert
                    between time zones.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Matching is by quantity.</strong> The combined disposal is matched against the combined
                    acquisition up to whichever quantity is smaller.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Leftover shares carry on.</strong> If more was sold than bought, the rest of the sale goes to the
                    <a href="HowItWorks/30-day-rule" class="link-styled">30-day rule</a> and then the
                    <a href="HowItWorks/section-104-pool" class="link-styled">Section 104 pool</a>. If more was bought than
                    sold, the extra shares can still be matched with a disposal in the previous 30 days. Anything left after
                    that goes into the pool.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>One combined trade, one disposal.</strong> On the Disposal Summary, a day's sells appear as a
                    single disposal. The individual broker trades are on the Imported Trades page. The exported
                    calculation file lists them under each disposal.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="card mb-4">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h2 class="h5 card-title">Worked example: two sells and a buy on the same day</h2>
                <p>Before 10 June 2024 you hold 100 shares in the Section 104 pool, with a total cost of £1,000. On 10 June 2024:</p>
                <div class="table-responsive">
                    <table class="table table-sm">
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Time</th><th>Trade</th><th class="text-end">Gross</th><th class="text-end">Fee</th><th class="text-end">Used by the app</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>09:00</td><td>Sell 40</td><td class="text-end">£600</td><td class="text-end">£5</td><td class="text-end">£595 net proceeds</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>11:00</td><td>Buy 50</td><td class="text-end">£700</td><td class="text-end">£10</td><td class="text-end">£710 cost</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>15:00</td><td>Sell 40</td><td class="text-end">£600</td><td class="text-end">£5</td><td class="text-end">£595 net proceeds</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <p>
                    The app combines the two sells into <strong>one disposal of 80 shares for £1,190</strong> net. The buy
                    becomes one acquisition of 50 shares costing £710. The buy came after the first sell, but that makes no
                    difference.
                </p>
                <div class="table-responsive">
                    <table class="table table-sm">
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Match</th><th class="text-end">Shares</th><th class="text-end">Proceeds</th><th class="text-end">Allowable cost</th><th class="text-end">Gain</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Same day</td><td class="text-end">50</td><td class="text-end">£1,190 × 50/80 = £743.75</td><td class="text-end">£710.00</td><td class="text-end">£33.75</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Section 104</td><td class="text-end">30</td><td class="text-end">£1,190 × 30/80 = £446.25</td><td class="text-end">£1,000 × 30/100 = £300.00</td><td class="text-end">£146.25</td></tr>
                            <tr class="fw-bold"><td>Total</td><td class="text-end">80</td><td class="text-end">£1,190.00</td><td class="text-end">£1,010.00</td><td class="text-end">£180.00</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <p class="mb-0">
                    Afterwards the pool holds 70 shares costing £700. The same-day purchase never enters the pool, because
                    the same-day match uses all of it. How the £10 of selling fees is split between the two matches is shown in
                    <a href="HowItWorks/net-proceeds" class="link-styled">Net proceeds and fees</a>.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </ChildContent>
    <FurtherReading>
        <li><a href="https://cgtvisualiser.co.uk/guides/same-day-rule/" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CGT Visualiser: same-day rule</a> (independent site)</li>
        <li><a href="https://github.com/alexpung/UK-Investment-tax-calculator/wiki/Share-matching" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Project wiki: share matching</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.gov.uk/hmrc-internal-manuals/capital-gains-manual/cg51560" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">HMRC CG51560</a>: the same-day and 30-day rules</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1992/12/section/105" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">TCGA 1992 s105</a></li>
    </FurtherReading>
</HowItWorksGuideLayout>
